ed for:","options":{"A":"Printing a string","B":"Buffered keyboard input (reading a string)","C":"Displaying a character","D":"Exiting"},"marks":1},{"id":"uni-futa-fsen102-0190","type":"mcq","stem":"The LABEL directive is used to:","options":{"A":"Define a jump target without allocating memory","B":"Allocate variables","C":"Define procedures","D":"End the program"},"marks":1},{"id":"uni-futa-fsen102-0191","type":"mcq","stem":"ORG 100h is used in:","options":{"A":"EXE programs","B":"COM programs (to set the origin to 100h)","C":"Device drivers","D":"Boot sectors"},"marks":1},{"id":"uni-futa-fsen102-0192","type":"mcq","stem":"In a COM program, how many segments are there?","options":{"A":"Separate code and data segments","B":"One segment (code, data, and stack share the same segment)","C":"Two segments","D":"Four segments"},"marks":1},{"id":"uni-futa-fsen102-0193","type":"mcq","stem":"The ASSUME directive tells the assembler:","options":{"A":"Which segment registers point to which segments","B":"The program size","C":"The stack size","D":"The entry point"},"marks":1},{"id":"uni-futa-fsen102-0194","type":"mcq","stem":"Which assembler is commonly used for 8086 assembly?","options":{"A":"NASM","B":"MASM (Microsoft Macro Assembler)","C":"GAS","D":"FASM"},"marks":1},{"id":"uni-futa-fsen102-0195","type":"mcq","stem":"The OFFSET operator returns:","options":{"A":"The segment address","B":"The offset (physical address within a segment) of a variable","C":"The size of a variable","D":"The value of a variable"},"marks":1},{"id":"uni-futa-fsen102-0196","type":"mcq","stem":"The PTR operator is used to:","options":{"A":"Override the type/size of an operand","B":"Get a pointer","C":"Print a value","D":"Push data"},"marks":1},{"id":"uni-futa-fsen102-0197","type":"mcq","stem":"MOV [BX], 5 is ambiguous because:","options":{"A":"BX cannot address memory","B":"The assembler doesn't know if 5 is a byte or word","C":"5 is too large","D":"BX is not initialized"},"marks":1},{"id":"uni-futa-